comments:
Played with this for a bit, and it seems like removing `OrthographicProjection` as a required component and replacing it with `Projection(|| Projection::Orthographic(OrthographicProjection::default_2d())),` works just fine.

I don't see an immediate need for making a special wrapper type for the ortho component if we can just continue to use `Projection`. I also don't see why we shouldn't just KISS and modify `Projection` to be

```rs
pub enum Projection {
    Orthographic {
        near: f32,
        far: f32,
        scale: f32,
        // ...
    },
    Perspective {
        near: f32,
        far: f32,
        fov: f32,
        // ...
    },
    // Can extend the set with other common projections like oblique.
    // Migration is self-documenting thanks to pattern matching.
    Custom(Box<dyn CameraProjection>)
}
```
with some ergonomic public methods. This would also simplify custom camera projections - there is no need for a generic `CameraProjectionPlugin<T>`, and no way for custom projections to conflict with the builtins or each other, it is completely unambiguous by looking at the value of `Projection`.

Alternatively, if we wanted to be, IMO, overly-general, we could do something like:

```rs
struct Projection(Box<dyn CameraProjection>);
```

Not a huge fan of this, because it makes life harder for 99.9% of use cases for no good reason (e.g. I just want to tweak the fov), so I'd prefer the first solution, which seems much more pragmatic.

As for accessing the projection contained in the custom variant, it should be possible to fallibly downcast into a type if the user knows the type they expect it to be.
